<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Swedish Ingria</h1><p class="st-meta">1617 CE – 1721 CE (approx.) · capital Narva</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swedish province at the head of the Gulf of Finland that cut Russia off from the Baltic, until Peter the Great built St Petersburg on its land.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> captured the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivangorod">Ivangorod</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingisepp">Jama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koporye">Koporye</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truce-of-plussa">Truce of Plussa</a> in 1583, but returned them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1595. During the Time of Troubles Sweden reconquered the area, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-stolbovo">Treaty of Stolbovo</a> in 1617 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> to Sweden permanently, cutting Russia off from the sea.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> was a thinly populated frontier, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> together with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Estonia</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> inhabitants left, and Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savonia">Savonia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karelians">Karelia</a> settled, forming the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingrian-finns">Ingrian Finns</a>. The fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyenskans">Nyenskans</a> and the town of Nyen at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a> controlled trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyenskans">Nyenskans</a> in 1703 and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St Petersburg</a> nearby. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> fell in 1704, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> in 1721 confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingria">Ingria</a> as Russian. It became the core of the St Petersburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/swedish-ingria.jpg" alt="Swedish Ingria" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: User: David Newton · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naval_ensign_of_Sweden.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1617 CE – 1721 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Narva</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity, Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Finnish, Swedish, Russian, Votic and Izhorian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ingermanland, Ingria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1669&amp;focus=swedish-ingria" aria-label="Swedish Ingria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5274 183 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Swedish Ingria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5594 439L5381 440M5594 439L5381 440M5594 439L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 440h0M5381 440h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5594 439h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5594 439h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1669&amp;focus=swedish-ingria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

